Comment (internal/authz/reconcile.go:134):
**[Medium] Concurrent boot can crash a replica: `Create` has no conflict handling.**

`MigrateUpWithConfig` (and thus this reconcile) runs on *every* `ccf run` replica boot — `cmd/run.go:87` calls it and treats any error as fatal (`sugar.Fatalw`). In an HA / rolling deploy, multiple replicas boot concurrently. When a config grant is newly added, two replicas can both `SELECT` the triple as absent (the `tx.Find` above) and then both reach this `tx.Create`. Under Postgres READ COMMITTED the second `INSERT` blocks on the first's uncommitted unique key, then raises a duplicate-key violation on commit. That error propagates out of the transaction → out of `ReconcileConfigRoleAssignments` → out of `MigrateUpWithConfig` → the replica exits fatally (CrashLoopBackOff).

It self-heals on the next restart (the row now exists, so this branch is skipped), so this is not data loss — but it can wedge/slow a rollout and emits fatal-level logs for a benign race.

Suggestion: make the insert idempotent so a losing race is a no-op rather than fatal:
```go
if err := tx.Clauses(clause.OnConflict{
    Columns:   []clause.Column{{Name: "assignee_type"}, {Name: "assignee_id"}, {Name: "role_name"}},
    DoNothing: true,
}).Create(&relational.CCFRoleAssignment{...}).Error; err != nil {
    return err
}
```
(or catch the unique-violation and treat it as benign). The `adopted`/`deleted` paths are already concurrency-tolerant; only this `Create` introduces a fatal conflict.
